          dealing: 'All cards dealt out equally to the players. Any remainder is stacked face up in the centre to begin the play pile. Each player holds their cards as a face-down deck.',
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        rules: {
          objective: 'Collect all the cards in the deck through suit matching.',
          gameplay: 'All cards are dealt out equally to the players; any remainder is stacked face up in the centre to start the play pile. Each player holds their cards as a face-down deck. Turn order is anti-clockwise, starting with the dealer. On your turn you reveal the top card of your deck and place it face up on the centre play pile. If your card matches the suit of the previous top card, you collect the entire play pile as your collected pile and get another turn immediately. If the suit does not match, your card stays on the pile and turn passes to the next player on your right. If your deck runs out you reshuffle your collected pile to form a new deck; if you have no collected pile you are eliminated. The last player left with all the cards wins.',
          keyRules: [
            'Deal all cards equally; any remainder starts the centre play pile.',
            'Turn order is anti-clockwise from the dealer.',
            'On your turn reveal your top card and place it on the centre play pile.',
            "If your card matches the previous card's suit, take the whole play pile and take another turn.",
            'If it does not match, leave the card on the pile and pass turn to the player on your right.',
            'Reshuffle your collected pile when your deck is empty; no collected pile means elimination.',
            'Last player with all the cards wins.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: "In Rang Milanti, pure randomness determines the cards you flip, but 'Endurance' is the key. Since it is a game of collection, you want to be the one who triggers a match when the pile is largest. There is no active choice in flipping, but players can speed up or slow down their flipping rhythm (house rules permitting) to 'Time' their cards against a large pile.",
          intermediate: "The '40-Card' Advantage: If playing with a 40-card deck (where 2s through 6s are removed), the density of each suit is slightly higher relative to the deck size. This means matches happen more frequently. Pay attention to which suits have been 'Buried' in other players' collected pilesâ€”if you see a lot of Spades being won by a rival, you know the remaining deck is 'Spade-Light', making a Spade match less likely soon.",
          advanced: "Social Momentum: The psychological aspect of Rang Milanti involves the 'Chain Win'. Because a winner gets another turn, they often hit a 'Hot Streak' where they match their own just-won card's suit. If you see a player on a streak, stay alertâ€”they are effectively thinning the deck's variety, making it easier for you to hit a match once their streak breaks.",
          tips: [
            "Always shuffle your collected pile thoroughly before flipping againâ€”otherwise, you'll flip the same patterns that just won you the pile.",
            'In a 2-player game, matches occur on average every 4 cardsâ€”be ready for fast-moving piles.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
